    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "yeah i'll check the node metrics but i'm worried it's something deeper. we have a pretty isolated setup. and like i said, it's just so middle of nowhere. nothing in the logs points to a specific container or node every time, it's just these tiny, inexplicable glitches that pop up and disappear. it's almost like there's some hidden interference or something just making the system act up when nobody's looking. any ideas for how to detect if something is actually \"interfering\" on a kernel level vs just standard heavy load?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "If standard resource monitoring isn't yielding results, you may need to capture kernel-level traces using tools like `perf` or eBPF. These advanced tracing tools allow you to sample system calls and kernel events during the test runs, potentially revealing low-level context switches, scheduling delays, or unexpected I/O wait states. Alternatively, try running the tests on a completely dedicated, lean machine to rule out any shared infrastructure noise, even if your setup *seems* isolated.",
      "source": "target",
